\subsection{Short English statement}
Develop reliable projected enclosures, distinguish isoresonant potentials, and classify operators with controlled resolvents.

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Derived a Schur-complement exclusion certificate and classified finite matrices with reciprocal-distance resolvent growth. No J-compatible infinite-dimensional scheme, isoresonance discriminator or full resolvent/counting classification.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{A projected-space exclusion certificate.}Let a bounded operator on an orthogonal sum be \(T=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{pmatrix}\), with the first block finite dimensional. If \(D-z\) is invertible, block elimination proves that \(T-z\) is invertible exactly when \(S(z)=A-z-B(D-z)^{-1}C\) is invertible. If \(A-z\) is invertible and
\[\|(A-z)^{-1}\|\,\|B\|\,\|(D-z)^{-1}\|\,\|C\|<1,
\]
then factoring \(S=(A-z)[I-(A-z)^{-1}B(D-z)^{-1}C]\) and summing a Neumann series certifies exclusion of \(z\) from the spectrum. A finite compression by itself omits the decisive complement resolvent.
\paragraph{A finite-dimensional classification test.}For a complex matrix, a bound \(\|(T-z)^{-1}\|\le M/\operatorname{dist}(z,\sigma(T))\) for every resolvent parameter holds if and only if the matrix is diagonalizable. Sufficiency follows from \(T=S\Lambda S^{-1}\), with \(M=\|S\|\|S^{-1}\|\). For necessity, a Jordan block of size \(r\ge2\) has resolvent term proportional to \((z-\lambda)^{-r}\); applying it to the last basis vector gives a norm bounded below by a positive constant times that power near \(\lambda\), even after fixed invertible similarity. This contradicts a reciprocal-distance bound.
\paragraph{Gap and conventions.}Neither argument uses or changes the source's antilinear conjugation \(J\). A useful \(J\)-compatible projection scheme must establish complement estimates respecting the actual structure. Infinite-dimensional polynomial resolvent classifications, compact-perturbation counting, and data distinguishing isoresonant or isospectral potentials do not follow from the finite-matrix test. All remain unresolved, including the exterior-disk classification, which is different from distance-to-spectrum control.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked block factorization order, strict Neumann inequality, Jordan-pole growth, similarity constants and the antilinear J convention. This was an LLM adversarial audit, not independent human verification; no theorem prover was used. Prompts A then B applied to this ID: Checked block factorization order, strict Neumann inequality, Jordan-pole growth, similarity constants and the antilinear J convention. Scope remains limited: Control the infinite-dimensional complement resolvent while preserving the source conjugation structure, then address the separate classification and resonance questions.

Coordinator checked the Schur-complement exclusion criterion and finite-dimensional Jordan-resolvent classification, with infinite-dimensional scope excluded. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

